  "question": "What are the two distinct causes of hallucination described in this corpus, and how do they differ?",
  "answer": "The two distinct causes of hallucination mentioned in the given text are:\n\n1. [hallucinations-due-to-model-outputs-being-too-confident::0]: This type of hallucination occurs when a model produces an output that is not based on any evidence or fact, but the model expresses such confidence in its output that it convinces itself and others as if it were true.\n\n2. [hallucinations-from-retrieval-or-contextualization-failures::1]: This kind of hallucination arises from errors during the retrieval or contextualization process where the model selects information that is irrelevant, incorrect, or outdated for a given question or task.\n\nThese two causes differ in their origin and mechanism:\n\n- The first one (chunk id 0) focuses on the model's confidence level. Even if the output is entirely fabricated, the model's high confidence can lead to it being accepted as true, both by the model itself and potentially by users.\n  \n- The second cause (chunk ids 1 and 2) involves errors in how information is retrieved or used contextually. This could be due to incorrect retrieval of data, using outdated information, or misinterpreting relevant evidence, leading to outputs that are factually inaccurate.\n\nBoth types can result in misleading or false information being generated by the model, but they differ in their specific mechanisms and points of failure within the AI system's operation.",
